Silver applies the rules. Lesson 22's `F.when` chain names the first rule a row breaks; a row that breaks none gets NULL. Each rule checks for missing values first, so a NULL can't slip through:

- both dates must be there, and the service can't be after the submission;
- `allowed_amount` is empty until the payer decides, which is fine; when it's there it can't be above `billed_amount`. A NULL comparison doesn't fire a `when`, so an empty `allowed_amount` passes;
- the status must be one of the five known ones.

Rows with no problem go to silver with bronze's columns (`select(bronze.columns)` drops the helper column). The rest go to quarantine with `_problem`. Every bronze row lands in exactly one of the two, so the counts must add up.
